1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:07,503
On the evening of the twenty-eighth of May, 1453, the bells of Constantinople rang out.

2
00:00:07,883 --> 00:00:19,025
The people of the city, Greeks and Italians, Orthodox and Catholic, who for months had refused to pray together, came in their thousands to the great church of Hagia Sophia.

3
00:00:19,405 --> 00:00:24,273
For almost a thousand years, it had been the largest church in the Christian world.

4
00:00:24,653 --> 00:00:29,187
That night, it may have been the last place in the city where anyone felt safe.

5
00:00:31,487 --> 00:00:35,000
Outside, beyond the land walls, the noise had stopped.

6
00:00:35,380 --> 00:00:41,661
For a day and a night, an army of tens of thousands had been fasting and resting in near silence.

7
00:00:42,041 --> 00:00:43,665
Its guns were loaded.

8
00:00:44,045 --> 00:00:45,638
Its ladders were ready.

9
00:00:46,018 --> 00:00:52,937
And somewhere among the tents, a young sultan, just twenty-one years old, was waiting for the hours to pass.

10
00:00:55,237 --> 00:01:00,453
Inside the church, the emperor is said to have asked those around him for forgiveness.

11
00:01:00,833 --> 00:01:02,524
Then he rode back to the walls.

12
00:01:02,904 --> 00:01:11,320
His name was Constantine, and he stood at the end of a line of rulers that ran back, through nearly fifteen centuries, to Augustus.

13
00:01:11,700 --> 00:01:16,345
He called himself what all of them had been called: Emperor of the Romans.

14
00:01:18,645 --> 00:01:23,792
By the next evening, the Roman Empire, the oldest state in Europe, would be gone.

15
00:01:24,172 --> 00:01:30,364
How could a city that had stood behind its walls for a thousand years fall in fifty-three days?

16
00:01:30,744 --> 00:01:32,949
Who was the young ruler who did it?

17
00:01:33,329 --> 00:01:36,355
And what really happened on the last day of Rome?

18
00:01:55,655 --> 00:02:00,859
To understand why so many people wanted this city, you have to see where it stands.

19
00:02:01,239 --> 00:02:05,926
Constantinople sat on a triangle of land at the edge of Europe.

20
00:02:06,306 --> 00:02:08,847
To the south lay the Sea of Marmara.

21
00:02:09,227 --> 00:02:13,028
To the north, a deep, sheltered harbour called the Golden Horn.

22
00:02:13,408 --> 00:02:22,218
And to the east, the Bosphorus, the narrow strait that separates Europe from Asia and connects the Mediterranean world to the Black Sea.

23
00:02:22,598 --> 00:02:29,096
Whoever held this point controlled the crossing between two continents and the sea route between two seas.

24
00:02:31,396 --> 00:02:42,475
The Roman emperor Constantine the Great refounded the old Greek town of Byzantion here and dedicated it as his new capital in the year 330.

25
00:02:42,855 --> 00:02:47,622
Within two centuries, it had become the largest and richest city in Europe.

26
00:02:48,002 --> 00:02:54,083
When the western half of the Roman Empire collapsed in the fifth century, the eastern half did not.

27
00:02:54,463 --> 00:02:59,450
It carried on, ruled from Constantinople, for another thousand years.

28
00:03:01,750 --> 00:03:08,056
We call that state the Byzantine Empire, but that is a name invented by later historians.

29
00:03:08,436 --> 00:03:11,077
The people who lived in it never used it.

30
00:03:11,457 --> 00:03:17,100
They called themselves Romans, Romaioi, and their state was simply the Roman Empire.

31
00:03:17,480 --> 00:03:27,426
They spoke Greek, they were Orthodox Christians, and they saw themselves as the direct continuation of the empire of Augustus and Constantine.

32
00:03:27,806 --> 00:03:29,536
In a real sense, they were.

33
00:03:31,836 --> 00:03:35,239
What protected this city, above all, were its walls.

34
00:03:35,619 --> 00:03:49,349
In the early fifth century, under the emperor Theodosius the Second, a new line of land walls was built across the peninsula, from the Sea of Marmara to the Golden Horn, a distance of nearly six kilometres.

35
00:03:49,729 --> 00:03:52,085
It was not one wall but a system.

36
00:03:52,465 --> 00:03:54,103
First came a broad moat.

37
00:03:54,483 --> 00:03:56,760
Behind it, a lower outer wall.

38
00:03:57,141 --> 00:04:03,686
And behind that, the great inner wall, around twelve metres high, studded with nearly a hundred towers.

39
00:04:05,986 --> 00:04:08,940
For a thousand years, these defences held.

40
00:04:09,320 --> 00:04:15,367
Huns, Avars, Persians, Arabs, Bulgars and Rus all came, and all went away again.

41
00:04:15,747 --> 00:04:18,832
Only once had the city been taken by storm.

42
00:04:19,212 --> 00:04:30,462
In 1204, the knights of the Fourth Crusade, who had set out to fight in the Holy Land, turned on Constantinople instead, broke in from the harbour side and sacked it.

43
00:04:30,842 --> 00:04:36,251
The empire was recovered in 1261, but it never recovered from the blow.

44
00:04:38,551 --> 00:04:42,391
By the fifteenth century, the empire had shrunk to almost nothing.

45
00:04:42,771 --> 00:04:47,604
A new power had risen in Anatolia and the Balkans: the Ottoman Turks.

46
00:04:47,984 --> 00:04:59,554
Step by step, they absorbed the lands around the city, until the Roman Empire was little more than Constantinople itself, a few islands, and some territory in southern Greece.

47
00:04:59,934 --> 00:05:03,241
The capital was now an island in an Ottoman sea.

48
00:05:05,541 --> 00:05:08,489
Inside the walls, the change was obvious.

49
00:05:08,869 --> 00:05:13,276
In its prime, the city may have had several hundred thousand inhabitants.

50
00:05:13,656 --> 00:05:19,165
By 1453, most estimates put it at around forty or fifty thousand.

51
00:05:19,545 --> 00:05:28,160
Travellers described whole districts of ruins, fields and orchards inside the walls, and villages clustered around old churches.

52
00:05:28,540 --> 00:05:30,559
The great palaces were crumbling.

53
00:05:30,939 --> 00:05:38,191
A century earlier, an empress had pawned the crown jewels to Venice, and no one had ever been able to buy them back.

54
00:05:38,571 --> 00:05:45,559
And yet the city still held something no army had ever taken from it: the idea that it was the centre of the world.

55
00:05:57,859 --> 00:06:05,019
In January 1449, a man of forty-three was proclaimed emperor at Mystras, in southern Greece.

56
00:06:05,399 --> 00:06:08,320
His name was Constantine Palaiologos.

57
00:06:08,700 --> 00:06:17,031
He had spent much of his life as a governor and soldier in the Peloponnese, fighting to hold together what was left of the empire there.

58
00:06:17,411 --> 00:06:20,968
He had been married twice, and both wives had died young.

59
00:06:21,348 --> 00:06:22,728
He had no children.

60
00:06:23,108 --> 00:06:31,994
When he arrived in Constantinople that spring, he inherited a treasury that was nearly empty and an empire that was barely an empire at all.

61
00:06:34,294 --> 00:06:38,118
We know surprisingly little about what Constantine was like.

62
00:06:38,498 --> 00:06:41,105
No reliable portrait of him survives.

63
00:06:41,485 --> 00:06:46,278
Most of what we know comes from a handful of chroniclers, some of whom knew him well.

64
00:06:46,658 --> 00:06:56,075
His closest friend, the courtier George Sphrantzes, later wrote a short chronicle of his own life, and it is one of our most valuable sources.

65
00:06:56,455 --> 00:07:06,293
What comes through in all of them is a man more experienced in war than in politics, stubborn, brave, and keenly aware of how weak his position was.

66
00:07:08,593 --> 00:07:15,421
Two years later, in February 1451, the Ottoman sultan Murad the Second died at Edirne.

67
00:07:15,801 --> 00:07:21,285
Murad had attacked Constantinople once before, in 1422, and failed.

68
00:07:21,665 --> 00:07:25,435
In his later years, he had preferred to leave the city alone.

69
00:07:25,815 --> 00:07:29,130
His heir was his son Mehmed, who was not yet nineteen.

70
00:07:31,430 --> 00:07:33,595
Mehmed had already been sultan once.

71
00:07:33,975 --> 00:07:40,908
In 1444, when he was twelve, his father stepped down in his favour, but the experiment failed.

72
00:07:41,288 --> 00:07:48,702
A crusader army invaded the Balkans, and Murad had to come back to lead the army, crushing the crusaders at Varna.

73
00:07:49,082 --> 00:07:57,556
Two years later, after a revolt by the janissaries, Murad took the throne back for good, and Mehmed was sent away to the provinces.

74
00:07:57,936 --> 00:08:03,256
That humiliation, historians often suggest, may help explain what came next.

75
00:08:05,556 --> 00:08:10,500
The young sultan was clever, well educated and intensely ambitious.

76
00:08:10,880 --> 00:08:17,648
Contemporaries said he read history and spoke several languages, although exactly how many is debated.

77
00:08:18,028 --> 00:08:20,875
He was also secretive and could be ruthless.

78
00:08:21,255 --> 00:08:29,863
Almost as soon as he took power, his infant half-brother was killed, a grim practice that would become standard in Ottoman succession.

79
00:08:30,243 --> 00:08:37,491
Mehmed made peace with his neighbours, confirmed old treaties, and gave every sign of wanting quiet on his frontiers.

80
00:08:39,791 --> 00:08:45,050
In Constantinople, some took this as a sign that the new sultan was weak.

81
00:08:45,430 --> 00:08:47,288
That was a serious mistake.

82
00:08:47,668 --> 00:09:00,979
In 1451, the emperor's envoys complained about payments for an Ottoman prince, Orhan, who lived as a kind of hostage in the city, and hinted that he might be released as a rival claimant to the throne.

83
00:09:01,359 --> 00:09:10,285
According to the Greek historian Doukas, the grand vizier Halil Pasha, who was the strongest voice for peace at the Ottoman court, was furious.

84
00:09:10,665 --> 00:09:21,846
He told them, in Doukas's account, that they were fools, that the new sultan was nothing like his gentle father, and that if they kept pushing, they would lose even what little they had.

85
00:09:24,146 --> 00:09:28,358
For Mehmed, Constantinople was more than a strategic prize.

86
00:09:28,738 --> 00:09:32,325
For centuries, Muslim armies had dreamed of taking it.

87
00:09:32,705 --> 00:09:38,327
Arab forces had besieged the city in the seventh and early eighth centuries and failed.

88
00:09:38,707 --> 00:09:50,375
A saying attributed to the Prophet Muhammad, and much repeated at the Ottoman court, promised that Constantinople would one day be conquered, and praised the commander and the army that would do it.

89
00:09:50,755 --> 00:09:59,445
Among those said to have died beneath its walls during the early Arab sieges was Abu Ayyub al-Ansari, a companion of the Prophet.

90
00:09:59,825 --> 00:10:03,857
The young sultan wanted to be the commander that saying described.

91
00:10:06,157 --> 00:10:09,886
Whatever happened in that meeting, Mehmed had found his reason.

92
00:10:10,266 --> 00:10:14,824
Within months, he began to prepare the single greatest project of his life.

93
00:10:15,204 --> 00:10:17,201
He would take Constantinople.

94
00:10:29,501 --> 00:10:40,990
In the spring of 1452, Ottoman workers began to arrive at a quiet stretch of the European shore of the Bosphorus, a few kilometres north of Constantinople.

95
00:10:41,370 --> 00:10:48,240
This was the narrowest point of the strait, where Europe and Asia are only about seven hundred metres apart.

96
00:10:48,620 --> 00:10:54,873
On the Asian side, there was already a small Ottoman fortress, built by Mehmed's great-grandfather.

97
00:10:55,253 --> 00:10:57,359
Now Mehmed would build its partner.

98
00:10:59,659 --> 00:11:02,968
The speed of the work astonished everyone who saw it.

99
00:11:03,348 --> 00:11:14,673
Thousands of labourers and masons cut stone, burned lime and raised walls, and the sultan's senior commanders were each made responsible for building one of the great towers.

100
00:11:15,053 --> 00:11:22,795
Stone was taken from wherever it could be found, including, the Greek chroniclers bitterly noted, the ruins of churches.

101
00:11:23,175 --> 00:11:28,070
In roughly four and a half months, by the end of August, the fortress was finished.

102
00:11:28,450 --> 00:11:32,168
Its three great towers still stand on the hillside today.

103
00:11:34,468 --> 00:11:36,623
The Ottomans called it Boğazkesen.

104
00:11:37,003 --> 00:11:41,188
The name can mean the Cutter of the Strait, but also the Throat-Cutter.

105
00:11:41,568 --> 00:11:49,561
Its cannons now covered the Bosphorus, and every ship sailing to or from the Black Sea was ordered to stop and pay a toll.

106
00:11:49,941 --> 00:11:56,714
It was a direct threat to the city's grain supply and to the trade of the Italian merchants who lived there.

107
00:11:59,014 --> 00:12:06,501
In November 1452, a Venetian captain named Antonio Rizzo tried to sail past without stopping.

108
00:12:06,881 --> 00:12:08,834
The fortress opened fire.

109
00:12:09,214 --> 00:12:12,233
A single stone ball struck his ship and sank it.

110
00:12:12,613 --> 00:12:15,657
Rizzo and his crew were taken to the sultan.

111
00:12:16,037 --> 00:12:21,603
The crew were executed, and Rizzo was impaled and left beside the road as a warning.

112
00:12:21,983 --> 00:12:28,579
The Venetians in Constantinople, who had hoped to stay neutral, now understood exactly what was coming.

113
00:12:30,879 --> 00:12:35,750
Meanwhile, a man had arrived at the sultan's court with an extraordinary offer.

114
00:12:36,130 --> 00:12:42,829
His name was Orban, or Urban, and he was a cannon founder, probably from Hungary or Transylvania.

115
00:12:43,209 --> 00:12:51,618
According to Doukas, he had first gone to Constantinople and offered his services to the emperor, who could not afford to pay what he asked.

116
00:12:51,998 --> 00:12:54,128
So he went to the Ottomans instead.

117
00:12:56,428 --> 00:12:59,756
Mehmed, the story goes, asked him a single question.

118
00:13:00,136 --> 00:13:04,604
Could he cast a gun large enough to break the walls of Constantinople?

119
00:13:04,984 --> 00:13:06,807
Orban said that he could.

120
00:13:07,187 --> 00:13:14,973
The sultan, who understood the value of new technology better than almost anyone of his age, gave him everything he needed.

121
00:13:17,273 --> 00:13:21,268
Over the winter, at Edirne, Orban cast his greatest gun.

122
00:13:21,648 --> 00:13:24,420
The accounts that describe it are remarkable.

123
00:13:24,800 --> 00:13:27,852
Its barrel was said to be around eight metres long.

124
00:13:28,232 --> 00:13:34,341
It fired stone balls that, by the writers' own estimates, weighed several hundred kilograms.

125
00:13:34,721 --> 00:13:41,990
When it was tested, Doukas claims, the sound could be heard for miles, and the ball buried itself deep in the earth.

126
00:13:42,370 --> 00:13:52,423
To move it the two hundred kilometres to Constantinople took, he says, sixty oxen and hundreds of men, who smoothed the road and built bridges ahead of it.

127
00:13:54,723 --> 00:13:57,891
Historians treat these numbers with some caution.

128
00:13:58,271 --> 00:14:01,171
Medieval writers often exaggerated.

129
00:14:01,551 --> 00:14:08,672
But there is no doubt that the Ottoman army was assembling the most powerful siege artillery the world had yet seen.

130
00:14:09,052 --> 00:14:14,746
The walls of Constantinople had been designed to stop men, rams and catapults.

131
00:14:15,126 --> 00:14:17,650
They had never faced anything like this.

132
00:14:29,950 --> 00:14:33,408
Constantine knew he could not defend the city alone.

133
00:14:33,788 --> 00:14:41,792
The only real hope was help from the Catholic West: from Venice, Genoa, the pope, the kings of Hungary and Aragon.

134
00:14:42,172 --> 00:14:44,162
But that help came with a price.

135
00:14:44,542 --> 00:14:48,927
For centuries, the Orthodox and Catholic churches had been divided.

136
00:14:49,307 --> 00:14:55,646
The West had made it clear that it expected the Greeks to accept union with Rome, on Rome's terms.

137
00:14:57,946 --> 00:15:05,805
On the twelfth of December 1452, in Hagia Sophia, the union of the churches was formally proclaimed.

138
00:15:06,185 --> 00:15:15,154
The ceremony was led by Cardinal Isidore, a Greek-born churchman who had come from Rome with a small force of about two hundred soldiers.

139
00:15:15,534 --> 00:15:18,520
The pope's name was read out in the liturgy.

140
00:15:18,900 --> 00:15:22,425
To many of the Orthodox faithful, this was a betrayal.

141
00:15:22,805 --> 00:15:28,864
Crowds gathered in protest, and many people refused to set foot in the great church afterwards.

142
00:15:31,164 --> 00:15:34,297
One phrase from those months has become famous.

143
00:15:34,677 --> 00:15:43,506
The historian Doukas attributes it to Loukas Notaras, the grand duke, the most senior official in the empire after the emperor.

144
00:15:43,886 --> 00:15:50,488
Better, he is supposed to have said, to see the turban of the Turks ruling in the city than the Latin mitre.

145
00:15:50,868 --> 00:15:54,502
Whether Notaras ever really said it, we cannot be sure.

146
00:15:54,882 --> 00:16:00,481
But it captures the bitterness that divided the city on the eve of its greatest danger.

147
00:16:02,781 --> 00:16:05,478
Help did come, though far less than hoped.

148
00:16:05,858 --> 00:16:15,816
The most important arrival was a Genoese soldier named Giovanni Giustiniani Longo, an experienced specialist in the defence of walled towns.

149
00:16:16,196 --> 00:16:25,741
In late January 1453, he sailed in with two ships and around seven hundred well-armed men, many recruited on his own initiative.

150
00:16:26,121 --> 00:16:30,623
Constantine gave him command of the defence of the land walls.

151
00:16:32,923 --> 00:16:34,336
Others were there too.

152
00:16:34,716 --> 00:16:42,879
There were Venetian merchants and sailors who decided to stay and fight, under their governor in the city, Girolamo Minotto.

153
00:16:43,259 --> 00:16:55,799
There were Catalans, Cretans, a few men from other parts of Europe, and even the Ottoman prince Orhan with his own followers, who had every reason to fight, since he could expect no mercy from Mehmed.

154
00:16:56,179 --> 00:16:57,889
Not everyone stayed, though.

155
00:16:58,269 --> 00:17:05,319
One night in late February, several ships slipped out of the harbour and sailed away, taking hundreds of people with them.

156
00:17:07,619 --> 00:17:11,880
The emperor ordered a careful count of every man able to fight.

157
00:17:12,260 --> 00:17:23,729
According to Sphrantzes, who carried out the count himself, the result was shocking: four thousand, seven hundred and seventy-three Greeks, and only about two hundred foreigners.

158
00:17:24,110 --> 00:17:27,422
Constantine told him to keep the number secret.

159
00:17:27,802 --> 00:17:35,311
Historians think the full force, including all the Italian contingents, was perhaps seven or eight thousand men.

160
00:17:35,691 --> 00:17:39,146
They had to defend around twenty kilometres of walls.

161
00:17:41,446 --> 00:17:45,033
Through the winter and early spring, the defenders prepared.

162
00:17:45,413 --> 00:17:48,089
The walls were repaired and the moat cleared.

163
00:17:48,469 --> 00:17:51,053
Weapons were collected and handed out.

164
00:17:51,433 --> 00:18:05,193
And at the start of April, a huge iron chain on wooden floats was stretched across the entrance of the Golden Horn, from the city to the walls of Galata on the other side, to keep the Ottoman fleet out of the harbour.

165
00:18:17,493 --> 00:18:21,459
Easter fell on the first of April in 1453.

166
00:18:21,839 --> 00:18:25,922
The next day, the first Ottoman troops appeared outside the walls.

167
00:18:26,302 --> 00:18:35,870
Over the following days, the main army arrived and spread out along the entire length of the land walls, from the Sea of Marmara to the Golden Horn.

168
00:18:36,250 --> 00:18:45,810
On the fifth or sixth of April, Mehmed himself pitched his great tent opposite the Gate of Saint Romanus, facing the weakest section of the defences.

169
00:18:48,110 --> 00:18:49,629
How big was the army?

170
00:18:50,009 --> 00:18:55,547
Christian eyewitnesses gave enormous figures, some of them in the hundreds of thousands.

171
00:18:55,927 --> 00:18:58,769
Those numbers are almost certainly too high.

172
00:18:59,149 --> 00:19:10,646
Modern historians generally estimate that the Ottoman force was somewhere between fifty and eighty thousand fighting men, with many more camp followers, labourers and volunteers.

173
00:19:11,026 --> 00:19:15,073
Even so, the defenders were outnumbered by perhaps ten to one.

174
00:19:17,373 --> 00:19:22,305
At the heart of the army were the janissaries, the sultan's elite infantry.

175
00:19:22,685 --> 00:19:33,411
They were originally recruited as boys from Christian families in the Balkans, converted to Islam, and trained as professional soldiers loyal only to the sultan.

176
00:19:33,792 --> 00:19:49,087
There were also the sipahis, cavalrymen who held land in return for military service, irregular troops and volunteers, Christian vassals from the Balkans, and specialists of every kind: engineers, miners, gunners and smiths.

177
00:19:51,387 --> 00:19:58,810
The guns were dragged into place and set up in batteries along the walls, protected by earthworks and wooden screens.

178
00:19:59,190 --> 00:20:05,234
The great bombard was positioned near the sultan's tent, aimed at the Gate of Saint Romanus.

179
00:20:05,614 --> 00:20:08,544
On the sixth of April, the bombardment began.

180
00:20:08,924 --> 00:20:12,846
Within days, it was continuous, day after day, for weeks.

181
00:20:15,146 --> 00:20:16,895
The giant gun was slow.

182
00:20:17,275 --> 00:20:23,086
Loading, aiming and cooling it took hours, and it could fire only a handful of times a day.

183
00:20:23,466 --> 00:20:27,377
But when its shots struck, whole sections of masonry collapsed.

184
00:20:27,757 --> 00:20:29,704
And it was only one of many.

185
00:20:30,084 --> 00:20:38,843
Dozens of smaller guns kept up a steady fire, and the walls, built to resist the weapons of the ancient world, began to crumble.

186
00:20:41,143 --> 00:20:44,492
Every night, the defenders went out to repair the damage.

187
00:20:44,872 --> 00:20:54,701
Under Giustiniani's direction, they filled the breaches with whatever they could find: earth, stones, timber, brushwood, barrels packed with soil.

188
00:20:55,081 --> 00:21:02,612
These improvised barricades turned out to absorb the impact of the cannonballs better than the old stone walls.

189
00:21:02,992 --> 00:21:07,139
By morning, a gap smashed open the day before would be blocked again.

190
00:21:19,439 --> 00:21:26,062
Under Islamic law and custom, a city was to be offered the chance to surrender before it was attacked.

191
00:21:26,442 --> 00:21:36,036
According to the chroniclers, Mehmed sent that offer at the start of the siege: the people's lives and property would be spared if the city opened its gates.

192
00:21:36,416 --> 00:21:38,123
The offer was refused.

193
00:21:38,503 --> 00:21:43,480
From that moment, if the city was taken by force, it could be given to the army.

194
00:21:45,780 --> 00:21:48,990
Mehmed began by clearing the land around the city.

195
00:21:49,370 --> 00:21:57,461
A small fortress at Therapia, on the Bosphorus, held out for two days under cannon fire before its garrison surrendered.

196
00:21:57,841 --> 00:22:02,210
Another small fort near the village of Studios fell within hours.

197
00:22:02,590 --> 00:22:07,642
According to Kritoboulos, the men who survived were impaled in front of the walls.

198
00:22:08,022 --> 00:22:16,822
On the island of Prinkipo in the Sea of Marmara, the defenders of a tower refused to surrender, and the Ottoman fleet set it on fire.

199
00:22:17,202 --> 00:22:19,521
The message to the city was clear.

200
00:22:21,821 --> 00:22:26,191
Inside the walls, the whole population was drawn into the defence.

201
00:22:26,571 --> 00:22:28,824
Men took their turns on the walls.

202
00:22:29,204 --> 00:22:31,234
Monks and priests carried stones.

203
00:22:31,614 --> 00:22:37,010
Women and children brought food and water to the fighters and helped to repair the damage.

204
00:22:37,390 --> 00:22:47,346
Constantine himself seems to have been everywhere, riding along the walls, inspecting the repairs, and spending the nights close to the most threatened sections.

205
00:22:49,646 --> 00:22:56,696
In mid-April, the Ottoman fleet made its first attempt to break the chain and force its way into the Golden Horn.

206
00:22:57,076 --> 00:22:59,680
The Christian ships were waiting behind it.

207
00:23:00,060 --> 00:23:07,516
From their high decks, the crews fired arrows and stones down onto the smaller galleys, and the attack was driven back.

208
00:23:07,896 --> 00:23:13,669
It was a small victory, but it showed that the chain, and the ships that guarded it, could hold.

209
00:23:15,969 --> 00:23:22,555
On the night of the eighteenth of April, Mehmed sent his troops against the damaged walls in the Lycus valley.

210
00:23:22,935 --> 00:23:24,393
The defenders held.

211
00:23:24,773 --> 00:23:29,265
The attackers were driven back after hours of fighting, with heavy losses.

212
00:23:29,645 --> 00:23:33,055
It was the first sign that this siege would not be quick.

213
00:23:35,355 --> 00:23:38,593
For the first two weeks, the defence seemed to be working.

214
00:23:38,973 --> 00:23:42,402
The walls were being damaged, but they were being repaired.

215
00:23:42,782 --> 00:23:44,899
The enemy fleet had been held.

216
00:23:45,279 --> 00:23:50,277
And the defenders still believed that help from the West might arrive at any moment.

217
00:23:50,657 --> 00:23:53,954
They had no idea what Mehmed was planning next.

218
00:24:06,254 --> 00:24:07,952
Mehmed also had a fleet.

219
00:24:08,332 --> 00:24:18,394
Eyewitnesses counted well over a hundred vessels, most of them galleys and smaller oared ships, under the command of an admiral named Baltaoğlu Süleyman.

220
00:24:18,774 --> 00:24:23,965
The fleet's task was to blockade the city from the sea and break into the Golden Horn.

221
00:24:24,345 --> 00:24:32,070
But the chain held, and behind it, the larger Christian sailing ships, with their high sides, were very difficult to attack.

222
00:24:34,370 --> 00:24:38,684
On the twentieth of April, the defenders saw sails on the horizon.

223
00:24:39,064 --> 00:24:51,326
Four ships were approaching from the south through the Sea of Marmara: three Genoese ships, sent with supplies and weapons with the support of the pope, and a large imperial transport carrying grain.

224
00:24:51,706 --> 00:24:55,583
Mehmed immediately ordered his fleet to stop them, whatever the cost.

225
00:24:57,883 --> 00:25:03,064
What followed was watched by thousands of people from the city walls and from the shore.

226
00:25:03,444 --> 00:25:07,498
The Ottoman galleys surrounded the four ships and tried to board them.

227
00:25:07,878 --> 00:25:18,479
The sailors on the tall ships fought back from above, hurling stones, javelins and arrows down onto the galleys, and pouring fire on any boat that came alongside.

228
00:25:18,859 --> 00:25:21,138
For a time, they kept moving forward.

229
00:25:23,438 --> 00:25:26,121
Then, close to the city, the wind dropped.

230
00:25:26,501 --> 00:25:32,069
The sails went slack, and the ships began to drift toward the shore where the sultan was watching.

231
00:25:32,449 --> 00:25:34,299
The Ottoman galleys closed in.

232
00:25:34,679 --> 00:25:41,402
The captains of the four ships lashed their vessels together, forming a single floating fortress, and fought on.

233
00:25:43,702 --> 00:25:54,843
Mehmed was so desperate to win that, according to several accounts, he rode his horse into the sea, shouting orders and abuse at his commanders, until his robes were soaked.

234
00:25:55,223 --> 00:25:58,569
On the walls, the people of Constantinople prayed.

235
00:26:00,869 --> 00:26:03,764
Late in the afternoon, the wind returned.

236
00:26:04,144 --> 00:26:08,359
The sails filled, and the four ships broke through the circle of galleys.

237
00:26:08,739 --> 00:26:14,231
As darkness fell, the chain was lowered, and they sailed safely into the Golden Horn.

238
00:26:14,611 --> 00:26:16,606
Not one of them had been lost.

239
00:26:18,906 --> 00:26:21,764
For the defenders, it was a wonderful moment.

240
00:26:22,144 --> 00:26:26,247
For Mehmed, it was a humiliation in front of his entire army.

241
00:26:26,627 --> 00:26:32,613
Admiral Baltaoğlu, who had been wounded in the eye during the battle, was stripped of his command.

242
00:26:32,993 --> 00:26:36,327
Some accounts say he was beaten in front of the sultan.

243
00:26:36,707 --> 00:26:38,705
Mehmed had learned a hard lesson.

244
00:26:39,085 --> 00:26:41,800
He could not win at sea in the ordinary way.

245
00:26:42,180 --> 00:26:44,914
He would need to do something no one expected.

246
00:26:57,214 --> 00:27:06,980
Across the Golden Horn from Constantinople stood Galata, also called Pera, a walled town that belonged to the Republic of Genoa.

247
00:27:07,360 --> 00:27:09,113
Officially, it was neutral.

248
00:27:09,493 --> 00:27:14,351
Its people traded with both sides, and many of them quietly helped the city.

249
00:27:14,731 --> 00:27:16,747
Mehmed left Galata alone.

250
00:27:17,127 --> 00:27:23,522
But behind its walls rose a range of steep hills, and the sultan had noticed something about them.

251
00:27:25,822 --> 00:27:37,806
In the days after the naval defeat, his engineers built a road from the shore of the Bosphorus, up over the hills behind Galata, and down to the waters of the Golden Horn on the far side.

252
00:27:38,186 --> 00:27:41,660
Wooden rails were laid along it and greased with fat.

253
00:27:42,040 --> 00:27:43,875
Wheeled cradles were built.

254
00:27:44,255 --> 00:27:49,392
And on the twenty-second of April, the Ottomans began to drag their ships overland.

255
00:27:51,692 --> 00:27:57,825
Teams of oxen and men hauled the galleys out of the water, onto the cradles and up the slope.

256
00:27:58,205 --> 00:28:09,234
Accounts describe the ships moving with their sails raised, with oarsmen at their benches pulling at the air as if they were rowing across the land, while drums and music played.

257
00:28:09,614 --> 00:28:14,485
Within a single day, a large part of the Ottoman fleet had crossed the hills.

258
00:28:14,864 --> 00:28:18,234
Most accounts put the number at around seventy ships.

259
00:28:20,534 --> 00:28:26,393
The defenders woke to find the enemy's ships floating inside their own harbour, behind the chain.

260
00:28:26,773 --> 00:28:34,177
The sea walls along the Golden Horn, which had been lightly guarded because they were thought to be safe, were now exposed.

261
00:28:34,557 --> 00:28:37,866
Men had to be taken from the land walls to defend them.

262
00:28:40,166 --> 00:28:42,815
The Venetians decided to strike back.

263
00:28:43,195 --> 00:28:53,243
On the night of the twenty-eighth of April, a small group of ships under a captain named Giacomo Coco tried to slip across the harbour and burn the Ottoman fleet.

264
00:28:53,623 --> 00:28:55,488
But the Ottomans were waiting.

265
00:28:55,868 --> 00:29:03,030
Their guns opened fire as soon as the ships were in range, and Coco's ship was hit and sank within moments.

266
00:29:03,410 --> 00:29:04,836
The attack collapsed.

267
00:29:05,216 --> 00:29:10,209
The Venetians later accused the Genoese of Pera of warning the sultan.

268
00:29:10,589 --> 00:29:12,158
The Genoese denied it.

269
00:29:12,538 --> 00:29:14,067
We do not know the truth.

270
00:29:16,367 --> 00:29:19,998
What followed was one of the darkest episodes of the siege.

271
00:29:20,378 --> 00:29:26,900
Sailors who had swum ashore were captured and executed by the Ottomans in full view of the walls.

272
00:29:27,280 --> 00:29:34,470
In response, the defenders executed Ottoman prisoners on the battlements, where the besieging army could see.

273
00:29:34,850 --> 00:29:41,011
Barbaro, the Venetian ship's doctor whose diary is one of our best sources, recorded both.

274
00:29:43,311 --> 00:29:56,131
Soon afterwards, Ottoman engineers built a floating bridge of barrels and planks across the upper part of the Golden Horn, so that troops and guns could move easily between the camp and the harbour walls.

275
00:29:56,511 --> 00:29:58,207
The noose was tightening.

276
00:30:10,507 --> 00:30:14,165
By May, the siege had settled into a grinding routine.

277
00:30:14,545 --> 00:30:16,355
By day, the guns fired.

278
00:30:16,735 --> 00:30:19,688
By night, the defenders repaired the breaches.

279
00:30:20,068 --> 00:30:26,446
Food was running short, and men on the walls had to leave their posts to find bread for their families.

280
00:30:26,826 --> 00:30:34,848
Tensions between Greeks, Venetians and Genoese grew worse, with each side accusing the others of not doing their share.

281
00:30:37,148 --> 00:30:39,818
The Ottomans kept testing the defences.

282
00:30:40,198 --> 00:30:45,341
In early May, they launched a heavy night attack near the Gate of Saint Romanus.

283
00:30:45,721 --> 00:30:52,171
A few days later, they struck at the walls near the Palace of Blachernae, at the northern end of the land walls.

284
00:30:52,551 --> 00:30:54,533
Both attacks were beaten back.

285
00:30:54,913 --> 00:31:03,900
Around this time, some of the emperor's advisers are said to have urged him to leave the city, to rally help from outside while there was still time.

286
00:31:04,280 --> 00:31:05,303
He refused.

287
00:31:05,683 --> 00:31:08,238
His place, he said, was with his people.

288
00:31:10,538 --> 00:31:12,964
Mehmed now tried a different approach.

289
00:31:13,344 --> 00:31:19,431
Among his troops were experienced silver miners from Serbia, from the mines of Novo Brdo.

290
00:31:19,811 --> 00:31:28,365
In mid-May, they began to dig tunnels toward the walls, aiming to get underneath them and collapse them, or to break into the city from below.

291
00:31:30,665 --> 00:31:33,128
The defenders had their own expert.

292
00:31:33,508 --> 00:31:41,881
He was an engineer known as Johannes Grant, whom Barbaro calls a German, although some historians have suggested he was Scottish.

293
00:31:42,261 --> 00:31:44,517
Grant organised the countermining.

294
00:31:44,897 --> 00:31:50,892
His men listened, in cellars and at the foot of the walls, for the faint sound of picks in the earth.

295
00:31:51,272 --> 00:31:55,399
Then they dug their own tunnels to meet the enemy underground.

296
00:31:57,699 --> 00:32:00,793
What followed was a terrifying war in the dark.

297
00:32:01,173 --> 00:32:09,250
When the tunnels met, the two sides fought hand to hand underground, by lamplight, in spaces barely wide enough to stand.

298
00:32:09,630 --> 00:32:17,040
The defenders flooded tunnels, filled them with smoke, or burned the wooden supports to bring the roof down on the miners.

299
00:32:17,420 --> 00:32:21,771
Over the following days, they found and destroyed tunnel after tunnel.

300
00:32:24,071 --> 00:32:26,439
There was another danger above ground.

301
00:32:26,819 --> 00:32:37,219
One morning, the defenders woke to find that a huge wooden siege tower had been built during the night in front of the walls, covered with hides to protect it from fire.

302
00:32:37,599 --> 00:32:40,867
Under its cover, the attackers were filling in the moat.

303
00:32:41,247 --> 00:32:46,095
That night, the defenders crept out and destroyed it with barrels of gunpowder.

304
00:32:48,395 --> 00:32:52,159
Meanwhile, the city waited for help that never seemed to come.

305
00:32:52,539 --> 00:33:02,229
In early May, a small ship had slipped out past the Ottoman fleet, its crew disguised, to search the Aegean for the relief fleet promised by Venice.

306
00:33:02,609 --> 00:33:05,118
On the twenty-third of May, it came back.

307
00:33:05,498 --> 00:33:07,316
The sailors had found nothing.

308
00:33:07,696 --> 00:33:18,262
According to Barbaro, they had discussed whether to sail on to safety, and decided instead that it was their duty to return and tell the emperor, whatever the cost.

309
00:33:20,561 --> 00:33:23,003
Constantine is said to have wept.

310
00:33:23,383 --> 00:33:24,769
There would be no fleet.

311
00:33:25,149 --> 00:33:26,695
There would be no crusade.

312
00:33:27,075 --> 00:33:30,257
Whatever happened now, the city would face it alone.

313
00:33:42,557 --> 00:33:46,627
The people of Constantinople lived surrounded by prophecies.

314
00:33:47,007 --> 00:33:54,064
Old texts and popular legends predicted the end of the city, and the end of the world, in many different ways.

315
00:33:54,444 --> 00:33:59,968
In the last week of May, as morale collapsed, people began to see signs everywhere.

316
00:34:00,348 --> 00:34:05,004
Several of them were written down by the chroniclers who lived through the siege.

317
00:34:07,304 --> 00:34:11,920
On the night of the twenty-second of May, there was a partial eclipse of the moon.

318
00:34:12,300 --> 00:34:13,995
This is not legend.

319
00:34:14,375 --> 00:34:17,197
Astronomers can calculate it precisely.

320
00:34:17,577 --> 00:34:24,623
For people who already believed that the end was near, the sight of the moon darkening over the city was terrifying.

321
00:34:26,923 --> 00:34:38,273
Soon afterwards, the clergy carried the city's most sacred icon, an image of the Virgin Mary believed to protect Constantinople, through the streets in a great procession.

322
00:34:38,653 --> 00:34:44,911
According to the accounts, the icon slipped from the hands of those carrying it and fell to the ground.

323
00:34:45,291 --> 00:34:48,834
It was so heavy that it took a long time to lift it again.

324
00:34:49,214 --> 00:34:58,419
Then a violent storm broke over the city, with thunder and hail and rain so heavy that the streets flooded, and the procession had to be abandoned.

325
00:35:00,719 --> 00:35:06,129
The next day, a thick fog covered the city, which was very unusual for late May.

326
00:35:06,509 --> 00:35:16,577
And that night, according to several writers, a strange light was seen around the dome of Hagia Sophia, moving slowly upwards and disappearing into the sky.

327
00:35:16,957 --> 00:35:21,095
Some took it to mean that the divine presence was leaving the city.

328
00:35:21,475 --> 00:35:23,780
We cannot know exactly what they saw.

329
00:35:24,160 --> 00:35:26,398
But we do know what they believed it meant.

330
00:35:28,698 --> 00:35:31,977
Outside the walls, Mehmed faced his own problems.

331
00:35:32,357 --> 00:35:34,689
The siege had lasted seven weeks.

332
00:35:35,069 --> 00:35:42,175
His soldiers were exhausted, and rumours spread that a Hungarian army or a Venetian fleet was on its way.

333
00:35:42,555 --> 00:35:50,421
At a council of war, the old grand vizier, Halil Pasha, argued that the siege should be lifted before disaster struck.

334
00:35:50,801 --> 00:35:56,022
Others, led by the commander Zaganos Pasha, insisted on a final assault.

335
00:35:58,322 --> 00:35:59,819
Mehmed chose to attack.

336
00:36:00,199 --> 00:36:11,669
According to some accounts, he had already sent a final offer to Constantine: if the city surrendered, the emperor could leave freely for the Peloponnese, and the people would be spared.

337
00:36:12,049 --> 00:36:19,653
Constantine is said to have replied that it was not in his power, or in the power of anyone in the city, to give it away.

338
00:36:20,033 --> 00:36:22,632
They would all die rather than surrender it.

339
00:36:24,932 --> 00:36:27,743
The date was set for the twenty-ninth of May.

340
00:36:28,123 --> 00:36:37,687
Heralds rode through the camp announcing that, by custom, the city would be given to the soldiers to plunder for three days if it was taken by storm.

341
00:36:38,067 --> 00:36:47,766
On the night of the twenty-seventh, the defenders on the walls saw the Ottoman camp light up with fires and torches, and heard the sound of drums and music.

342
00:36:48,146 --> 00:36:50,705
The next day, everything fell silent.

343
00:37:03,005 --> 00:37:05,406
The twenty-eighth of May was a Monday.

344
00:37:05,786 --> 00:37:09,218
In the Ottoman camp, the soldiers fasted and prayed.

345
00:37:09,598 --> 00:37:14,827
Mehmed rode along the lines, inspecting the troops and speaking to the commanders.

346
00:37:15,207 --> 00:37:18,757
Ladders, shields and siege equipment were brought forward.

347
00:37:19,137 --> 00:37:20,961
The guns were moved closer.

348
00:37:21,341 --> 00:37:25,373
In the city, the defenders knew exactly what this silence meant.

349
00:37:27,673 --> 00:37:34,153
Inside the walls, the bells rang, and icons and relics were carried along the walls in procession.

350
00:37:34,533 --> 00:37:44,285
People of every class took part, Greeks and Italians, Orthodox and Catholic, singing hymns and calling out the Kyrie eleison, Lord have mercy.

351
00:37:44,665 --> 00:37:51,725
According to the accounts, Constantine gathered his commanders and the leaders of the Italians and spoke to them.

352
00:37:54,025 --> 00:38:07,627
The longest version of his speech that survives comes from a chronicle written more than a century later, which drew on Sphrantzes's work but greatly expanded it, and historians treat its wording with suspicion.

353
00:38:08,007 --> 00:38:11,588
But the shorter contemporary accounts agree on its spirit.

354
00:38:11,968 --> 00:38:17,229
He asked his men to fight for their faith, their country, their families and their emperor.

355
00:38:17,609 --> 00:38:19,761
He thanked the Italians for staying.

356
00:38:20,141 --> 00:38:24,109
And he told them that, whatever happened, he would stand with them.

357
00:38:26,409 --> 00:38:30,191
Then, as night fell, came the service in Hagia Sophia.

358
00:38:30,571 --> 00:38:35,874
For the first time in months, Orthodox and Catholics prayed together under the great dome.

359
00:38:36,254 --> 00:38:43,049
The emperor took communion, and, we are told, asked forgiveness of everyone present for any wrong he had done them.

360
00:38:43,429 --> 00:38:49,346
Then he rode to the Palace of Blachernae, said farewell to his household, and went out to the walls.

361
00:38:51,646 --> 00:38:56,092
The gates between the inner and outer walls were locked behind the defenders.

362
00:38:56,472 --> 00:38:57,972
There would be no retreat.

363
00:38:58,352 --> 00:39:04,255
Most of the men had now taken their places on the outer wall and the stockades, where they would fight.

364
00:39:04,635 --> 00:39:17,097
Giustiniani and his Genoese, with many of the best Greek troops, held the most dangerous section, the Lycus valley, where the walls had been smashed and replaced with barricades of earth and timber.

365
00:39:19,397 --> 00:39:25,402
At about half past one in the morning of Tuesday, the twenty-ninth of May, the silence ended.

366
00:39:37,702 --> 00:39:40,564
The attack began with a terrifying noise.

367
00:39:40,944 --> 00:39:48,687
Drums, trumpets, cymbals and the battle cries of tens of thousands of men rose all at once along the walls.

368
00:39:49,067 --> 00:39:52,523
In the city, the church bells began to ring in alarm.

369
00:39:52,903 --> 00:39:56,170
People who were not fighting ran to the churches to pray.

370
00:39:56,550 --> 00:40:02,855
The assault came at many points at the same time, but Mehmed's real target was the Lycus valley.

371
00:40:05,155 --> 00:40:09,939
The first wave was made up largely of irregular troops and volunteers.

372
00:40:10,319 --> 00:40:16,863
They were poorly armed but numerous, and they threw themselves at the barricades with ladders for about two hours.

373
00:40:17,243 --> 00:40:22,101
The defenders cut them down with crossbows, handguns, arrows and stones.

374
00:40:22,481 --> 00:40:27,572
When the survivors fell back, the defenders were exhausted, but the line had held.

375
00:40:29,872 --> 00:40:35,207
The second wave came immediately afterwards: disciplined regular troops from Anatolia.

376
00:40:35,587 --> 00:40:44,230
They pressed hard against the barricades, and at one point, after a cannonball smashed through part of the stockade, a few hundred of them broke in.

377
00:40:44,610 --> 00:40:46,748
They were surrounded and driven out.

378
00:40:47,128 --> 00:40:48,752
Again, the line held.

379
00:40:51,052 --> 00:40:53,786
By now, the sky was beginning to lighten.

380
00:40:54,166 --> 00:40:56,774
The defenders had been fighting for hours.

381
00:40:57,154 --> 00:41:01,165
Then Mehmed sent in his last and best troops: the janissaries.

382
00:41:01,545 --> 00:41:06,870
They advanced in good order, to the sound of military music, fresh and disciplined.

383
00:41:07,250 --> 00:41:12,144
The sultan himself is said to have led them as far as the edge of the moat.

384
00:41:14,444 --> 00:41:20,211
For about an hour, the fighting at the barricade was as fierce as anything in the whole siege.

385
00:41:20,591 --> 00:41:24,972
Then, at the most critical moment, Giustiniani was seriously wounded.

386
00:41:25,352 --> 00:41:28,374
Accounts differ about how, and where he was hit.

387
00:41:28,754 --> 00:41:37,047
What is clear is that he was carried away from the wall, through a gate in the inner wall, and down to one of the Genoese ships in the harbour.

388
00:41:39,347 --> 00:41:42,017
For his men, the effect was catastrophic.

389
00:41:42,397 --> 00:41:50,039
Seeing their commander carried off, many of the Genoese thought the battle was lost, and they began to follow him through the gate.

390
00:41:50,419 --> 00:41:53,738
The defence of the stockade began to break apart.

391
00:41:54,118 --> 00:42:08,982
At almost the same moment, according to Doukas, a group of Ottoman soldiers found a small gate further north, called the Kerkoporta, which had been left unlocked, entered the space between the walls, and raised their banners on a tower.

392
00:42:09,362 --> 00:42:11,719
Some historians accept this story.

393
00:42:12,099 --> 00:42:15,508
Others doubt it, because it appears in only one source.

394
00:42:15,888 --> 00:42:20,601
But either way, the cry soon spread along the walls: the city is taken.

395
00:42:22,902 --> 00:42:25,347
The janissaries poured over the barricades.

396
00:42:25,727 --> 00:42:30,949
The defenders on the outer walls were trapped between the walls, and many were killed there.

397
00:42:31,329 --> 00:42:38,285
Elsewhere along the walls, as news spread, defenders abandoned their positions to try to reach their families.

398
00:42:38,665 --> 00:42:42,404
Within a short time, the Ottoman army was inside the city.

399
00:42:44,704 --> 00:42:45,673
And the emperor?

400
00:42:46,053 --> 00:42:50,303
The truth is that nobody knows exactly how Constantine died.

401
00:42:50,683 --> 00:43:03,010
The most widely repeated account says that when he saw that the battle was lost, he threw away his imperial insignia and charged into the enemy with a few companions, and was never seen again.

402
00:43:03,390 --> 00:43:09,136
Some writers said his body was identified later by the imperial eagles on his boots.

403
00:43:09,516 --> 00:43:11,320
Others told different stories.

404
00:43:11,700 --> 00:43:15,340
His body was never certainly found, and no tomb exists.

405
00:43:17,640 --> 00:43:20,103
It did not take long for legends to grow.

406
00:43:20,483 --> 00:43:23,747
In Greek folklore, the emperor never died at all.

407
00:43:24,127 --> 00:43:33,666
An angel turned him to marble and hid him in a cave beneath the city, where he sleeps, waiting for the day he will wake and take Constantinople back.

408
00:43:34,046 --> 00:43:36,122
They called him the Marble Emperor.

409
00:43:48,422 --> 00:43:50,193
What followed was the sack.

410
00:43:50,573 --> 00:43:55,471
Soldiers spread through the city, looting houses, churches and monasteries.

411
00:43:55,851 --> 00:44:00,289
Many people were killed in the first hours, especially near the walls.

412
00:44:00,669 --> 00:44:03,479
But most of the population was not massacred.

413
00:44:03,859 --> 00:44:04,975
They were captured.

414
00:44:05,355 --> 00:44:11,929
Men, women and children were roped together and taken away to be sold as slaves or held for ransom.

415
00:44:12,309 --> 00:44:18,523
Kritoboulos, a Greek who later served Mehmed, puts the number of captives at around fifty thousand.

416
00:44:18,903 --> 00:44:25,093
Whatever the exact figure, almost the entire population of the city lost its freedom that day.

417
00:44:27,393 --> 00:44:38,493
Thousands had taken refuge in Hagia Sophia, believing, according to one tradition, that an angel would come down to save the city when the enemy reached the great church.

418
00:44:38,873 --> 00:44:43,550
When the soldiers broke down the doors, the people inside were taken captive.

419
00:44:45,850 --> 00:44:52,844
In the harbour, sailors cut through the chain and some of the Italian ships escaped, crowded with refugees.

420
00:44:53,224 --> 00:44:57,391
Barbaro was on one of them, and so he lived to write his diary.

421
00:44:57,771 --> 00:45:01,264
Cardinal Isidore escaped disguised as a slave.

422
00:45:01,644 --> 00:45:04,411
Prince Orhan was killed trying to escape.

423
00:45:04,791 --> 00:45:10,018
Sphrantzes was captured, but was later ransomed, and lived for many years.

424
00:45:12,318 --> 00:45:15,076
In the afternoon, Mehmed entered the city.

425
00:45:15,456 --> 00:45:17,463
He rode to Hagia Sophia.

426
00:45:17,843 --> 00:45:24,505
Accounts say that he dismounted, knelt, and placed a handful of earth on his turban as a sign of humility.

427
00:45:24,885 --> 00:45:31,569
When he found a soldier breaking up the marble floor, he struck him and ordered that the building should not be damaged.

428
00:45:31,949 --> 00:45:35,044
Then he ordered that the church be turned into a mosque.

429
00:45:35,424 --> 00:45:40,597
On the following Friday, the first of June, prayers were held there for the first time.

430
00:45:42,897 --> 00:45:57,713
There is a famous story that Mehmed, walking through the ruins of the old imperial palace, recited a few lines of Persian poetry about the spider weaving curtains in the palace of the Caesars, and the owl calling from the towers.

431
00:45:58,093 --> 00:46:02,841
The story is told by later Ottoman writers, and we cannot be sure it happened.

432
00:46:03,221 --> 00:46:05,840
But it may tell us how he saw what he had done.

433
00:46:06,220 --> 00:46:08,252
He was not destroying an empire.

434
00:46:08,632 --> 00:46:10,025
He was inheriting one.

435
00:46:12,325 --> 00:46:20,420
In the days that followed, the sultan stopped the sack, earlier than the three days that custom allowed, according to some accounts.

436
00:46:20,800 --> 00:46:29,192
Loukas Notaras, who had survived, was at first treated with respect, but within days he was executed along with his sons.

437
00:46:29,572 --> 00:46:33,165
The Venetian governor Minotto was also executed.

438
00:46:33,545 --> 00:46:36,717
And then Mehmed set out to make the city great again.

439
00:46:39,017 --> 00:46:48,122
He brought in settlers from across his empire, Muslims, Christians and Jews alike, and in some cases forced whole communities to move there.

440
00:46:48,502 --> 00:47:01,820
He appointed a new Orthodox patriarch, the scholar Gennadios Scholarios, who had been one of the fiercest opponents of the union with Rome, and gave him authority over the empire's Orthodox Christians.

441
00:47:02,200 --> 00:47:05,512
He built a new palace, mosques, markets and baths.

442
00:47:05,892 --> 00:47:07,730
And he took a new title.

443
00:47:08,110 --> 00:47:13,335
Among his many titles, he called himself Kayser-i Rum, the Caesar of Rome.

444
00:47:25,635 --> 00:47:27,223
How do we know any of this?

445
00:47:27,603 --> 00:47:35,406
The fall of Constantinople is one of the best-documented events of the Middle Ages, but it is not as simple as it looks.

446
00:47:35,786 --> 00:47:45,419
Almost everything we know comes from a small group of writers, and each of them saw the siege from a different place, at a different time, and for different reasons.

447
00:47:47,719 --> 00:47:54,523
Nicolò Barbaro was a Venetian ship's doctor who kept a diary, day by day, through the whole siege.

448
00:47:54,903 --> 00:47:58,385
He is precise about dates and events in the harbour.

449
00:47:58,765 --> 00:48:06,225
But he is fiercely loyal to Venice, quick to praise Venetians and to blame the Genoese for almost everything that went wrong.

450
00:48:06,605 --> 00:48:14,304
Leonard of Chios, a Catholic archbishop who was also in the city, wrote a long letter to the pope soon after its fall.

451
00:48:14,684 --> 00:48:22,500
He was bitter about the Greeks, many of whom had rejected the union of the churches, and he blamed them for their own disaster.

452
00:48:24,800 --> 00:48:32,153
On the Greek side, there is Sphrantzes, the emperor's friend, whose short chronicle is personal and grief-stricken.

453
00:48:32,533 --> 00:48:43,945
There is Doukas, a Greek in the service of the Genoese, who wrote a vivid history a few years later and recorded many of the most dramatic stories, including the Kerkoporta.

454
00:48:44,325 --> 00:48:51,214
There is Laonikos Chalkokondyles, an Athenian historian who wrote about the rise of the Ottomans.

455
00:48:51,594 --> 00:49:00,449
And there is Kritoboulos of Imbros, who wrote his history for Mehmed himself, and presents the sultan as a heroic new Alexander.

456
00:49:02,749 --> 00:49:14,778
From the Ottoman side, the most important early source is Tursun Beg, an official who was present at the siege as a young man and wrote about it decades later in praise of the sultan.

457
00:49:15,158 --> 00:49:24,280
There is also a famous Slavonic account attributed to a man named Nestor-Iskander, who claims to have been a Christian serving in the Ottoman army.

458
00:49:24,660 --> 00:49:29,349
How much of it is genuine eyewitness testimony is still debated.

459
00:49:31,649 --> 00:49:36,455
That is why historians argue about the numbers, the dates and the details.

460
00:49:36,835 --> 00:49:40,047
When the sources agree, we can be fairly confident.

461
00:49:40,427 --> 00:49:44,558
When a dramatic story appears in only one of them, we should be careful.

462
00:49:44,938 --> 00:49:53,792
And when an account was written long after the event, or for a patron with a reason to shape the story, we have to ask why it was told the way it was.

463
00:49:54,172 --> 00:50:00,616
The story in this film follows the sources as closely as possible, and tells you when they disagree.

464
00:50:12,916 --> 00:50:17,486
The news reached Venice, Rome and the courts of Europe within weeks.

465
00:50:17,866 --> 00:50:19,548
The shock was enormous.

466
00:50:19,928 --> 00:50:25,098
The pope called for a crusade to take the city back, but no crusade ever came.

467
00:50:25,478 --> 00:50:39,007
Within a few years, the last Byzantine territories also fell: the Peloponnese in 1460, and the Empire of Trebizond, a separate Greek state on the Black Sea coast, in 1461.

468
00:50:41,307 --> 00:50:50,544
You may have heard that the fall of Constantinople caused the Renaissance, as Greek scholars fled to Italy, carrying ancient manuscripts with them.

469
00:50:50,924 --> 00:50:52,676
That is only partly true.

470
00:50:53,056 --> 00:51:02,134
Greek scholars had been teaching in Italy for more than half a century before 1453, and the Renaissance was already well under way.

471
00:51:02,514 --> 00:51:06,260
The refugees added to a movement that already existed.

472
00:51:06,640 --> 00:51:07,962
They did not start it.

473
00:51:10,262 --> 00:51:18,477
You may also have heard that it closed the trade routes to Asia and forced Europeans to sail around the world, leading to Columbus.

474
00:51:18,857 --> 00:51:23,601
That idea is very popular, and historians have largely rejected it.

475
00:51:23,981 --> 00:51:33,062
Trade with the East continued through the Ottoman Empire, and the Portuguese were exploring the African coast long before 1453.

476
00:51:35,362 --> 00:51:38,234
So why does 1453 matter?

477
00:51:38,614 --> 00:51:46,137
Because on that day, a state that could trace its history back, without a break, to the Rome of Augustus came to an end.

478
00:51:46,517 --> 00:51:52,272
Many historians have used it as one of the dates that mark the end of the Middle Ages.

479
00:51:52,652 --> 00:52:03,231
It was also a clear sign of a new age of warfare: the walls that had defended the city for a thousand years had been broken, in large part, by gunpowder.

480
00:52:03,611 --> 00:52:06,309
And it marked the rise of a new great power.

481
00:52:06,689 --> 00:52:11,714
For almost five centuries, the city would be the capital of the Ottoman Empire.

482
00:52:14,014 --> 00:52:16,629
It was never really a city of endings.

483
00:52:17,009 --> 00:52:22,159
The Ottomans called it Kostantiniyye, the city of Constantine, for centuries.

484
00:52:22,539 --> 00:52:30,278
Over time, another name came into common use, one that probably comes from a Greek phrase meaning, simply, to the city.

485
00:52:30,658 --> 00:52:33,873
In 1930, it became the official name.

486
00:52:34,253 --> 00:52:35,225
Istanbul.

487
00:52:37,525 --> 00:52:42,355
And if you go there today, you can still walk along the land walls of Theodosius.

488
00:52:42,735 --> 00:52:48,997
Large stretches of them still stand, battered, patched, and half covered with grass and fig trees.

489
00:52:49,377 --> 00:52:55,407
In the valley of the Lycus, near the place where the barricades stood, the stones are still broken.

490
00:52:57,707 --> 00:53:03,877
As far as we know, Constantine Palaiologos and Mehmed the Conqueror never met face to face.

491
00:53:04,257 --> 00:53:09,092
For fifty-three days, they faced each other across a few hundred metres of ground.

492
00:53:09,472 --> 00:53:11,001
One lost everything.

493
00:53:11,381 --> 00:53:14,671
The other became one of the most famous rulers in history.

494
00:53:15,051 --> 00:53:23,437
And between them, on a spring morning in 1453, the Roman Empire, after almost fifteen hundred years, came to an end.

